<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">May There Always Be Sunshine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Пусть всегда будет солнце!, Pust' vsegda budet solntse!</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;May There Always Be Sunshine&quot;, also known as &quot;Solnechny krug&quot; (The Circle of the Sun), is a Soviet children's song of 1962 with music by Arkady Ostrovsky and words by Lev Oshanin. The writer and children's poet Korney Chukovsky later wrote that it grew from the four lines of its refrain, made up in 1928 by a four-year-old boy, Kostya Barannikov.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song was first performed by Maya Kristalinskaya on the radio show Good Morning! in July 1962. Tamara Miansarova sang it at the Sopot International Song Festival in 1963 and won first prize there, after which it became popular throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and abroad. Young Pioneers sang it at their camps and school meetings, as did Little Octobrists in primary schools and even children below school age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The words speak of a longing for lasting peace among millions of people, young and old. In war, the song holds, there are no winners, and for the sake of the people and above all their children peace must be won and the dark clouds that cover the sun removed. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> it was widely regarded as expressing the people's desire for peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1964 the Swedish band Hootenanny Singers, led by the future ABBA member Björn Ulvaeus, released Gabrielle, based on the song, and had a success in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> that gave the band its first international exposure. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> sang it in English, and it appears on Together in Concert, recorded with Arlo Guthrie in 1975. Ilse and Hans Naumilkat and Manfred Streubel made a German version, Immer lebe die Sonne, sung by the Ernst Thälmann Pioneers; Finnish Pioneers sang it as Paistaa aurinko aina, and Estonian Pioneers in a translation by Heljo Mänd, best known in the recording by Georg Ots. Gidi Koren wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> words for his group The Brothers &amp; the Sisters.</p><p>The Canadian children's musicians Charlotte Diamond, on her album 10 Carrot Diamond of 1985, and Raffi sang the chorus in several languages. The song was part of a medley at the opening ceremony of the 2014 Olympic Winter Games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a>, and its title is inscribed on the pedestal of a bronze monument to Samantha Smith at Artek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “May There Always Be Sunshine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
